# A DATA FRAME, as a model of the Doctrine.
#
# The thesis: you should never write a loop over rows.  You write a
# program on ONE ROW — a stack of scalars, ordinary Braid — and a MODEL
# lifts it to the whole frame.  `List` appears nowhere in the row
# programs below.  What does the lifting is `with Frame`, and `Frame` is
# a model of the same `Doctrine` that `examples/circuits.braid` models:
# hom-object a FUNCTION BETWEEN COLUMNS, `embed` = `map`, `compose` =
# composition.  A row program of any width transports, because the
# hom-object's two arguments are whole STACKS — "the other columns ride
# past" is what the base's own `...` already means, and since
# 2026-09-16 there is no strength slot saying it again.
#
# So the functor laws of `map` are not something this file promises.
# They are the doctrine's seven arrow laws, and the audit runs them
# before anything below prints.
#
# WHAT IS A FRAME, exactly.  A frame is a map from an INDEX to a row —
# `Fn⟨I ⇒ row⟩` together with its key set.  This file represents it as
# `List(row)` with an IMPLICIT POSITIONAL INDEX: row i is the i-th
# element, the key set is `0 … n−1`, and `Fin(n) ⇒ row` stored
# tabulated and flat is exactly what a list is here (MANUAL §5, §13).
# The reading matters even though it is implicit, because it is what
# says WHY the model is a functor: `map` preserves the index pointwise,
# so a transported row program cannot move a row, cannot drop one, and
# cannot see its neighbours.  Every operation that does any of those —
# `keep`, `groupBy`, `sortBy`, `join`, the aggregations — is written
# ONCE, at frame level, outside the scope.  A functor cannot drop rows;
# that is the whole division of labour in this file.
#
# COLUMN NAMES ARE WORDS.  `data Trades = (sym: Str, px: Float, qty:
# Int)` generates `sym`, `px` and `qty` as ordinary definitions
# (2026-09-14, MANUAL §5).  Nothing in the type system knows a column
# is called `px` — the header is runtime data, carried as a `List(Str)`
# to the printer — and that is deliberate: a column name is a word, so
# it composes, quotes and reflects like every other word.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The carrier and the model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The arrow inside the carrier is `=Recursive>` for one reason: it is
# built from `map` over a `List`, and the prelude's list walkers are
# written with the knot.  The walk is structural and terminates, so the
# label is conservative rather than wrong; a bounded fold, Church-style
# the way the prelude's `fold` is, would drop it.
# `examples/circuits.braid` carries the same label for the same kind of
# reason.

## `Col(ρ, σ)` is a function from the ρ-columns to the σ-columns, and
## ρ and σ are STACKS (2026-09-16): a row program takes a whole row and
## leaves a whole row.  The ROW STORE inside is a `List(Box(ρ))` — one
## list of boxed rows — and NOT a stack of lists, which is the honest
## reason this type is written the way it is: a stack of lists is the
## non-injective column store (three columns of ten and ten columns of
## three are the same stack of Ints) and it must not enter the type.
## The box is a representation choice; `Col`'s arguments are the bare
## stacks, which is what the doctrine's `k(..., ...)` asks for.
data Col(a..., b...) = Fn⟨List(Box(a)) =Recursive> List(Box(b))⟩

## run a column function on a boxed frame — the carrier's own spelling
def runColB = (c rows -> c ; unCol ; _ rows ; ev)

## run a column function on a frame — the base word, used outside every
## `with Frame` scope.  It boxes on the way in and unboxes on the way
## out, so a one-column-in, one-column-out program (which is every one
## below) reads exactly as it did.
def runCol =
    c rows ->
    c ; unCol ; _ (rows ; [Box] ... ; map) ; ev ; [unBox] ... ; map

## embed: a row word becomes a column word.  This IS `map`, and saying
## so is the model's defining equation.
def cembed = (f -> [(rows -> rows ; [(e -> e ; unBox ; f ... ; ev ; Box)] ... ; map)] ; Col)

## compose: run one column function, then the next
def ccompose = (f g -> [(rows -> f rows ; runColB ; (mid -> g mid ; runColB))] ; Col)

## the audit's evidence: a fixed three-row frame, and a column function
## observed by summing what it produces there
def sample3   = 1 (2 (3 nil ; cons) ; cons) ; cons
def cobserve  = (c -> (c sample3 ; runCol) ; sum)

theory Columns(k(..., ...)) in Doctrine =
    embed   : Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ =Recursive> k(a, b)
    compose : k(a, b) k(b, c) =Recursive> k(a, c)
    observe : k(Int, Int) =Recursive> Int
    sample  : • =Recursive> k(Int, Int)

model Frame in Columns(Col) =
    embed   = cembed
    compose = ccompose
    observe = cobserve
    sample  = [dup ; +] ; cembed

# All FOUR doctrine slots are declared, so all FIVE of its laws run —
# leftId, rightId, assoc, embedFunctor, embedWide — and they are
# exactly the functor laws of `map` read at the hom-object.  They run before this file prints anything, and a
# model whose `compose` is wrong stops the file with
# `law 'leftId' fails for model Frame`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Transport, checked on Ints before a single trade is loaded
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every stage embeds on its own, at the width it is written: `embed
# [dup]` is `Col(Int, Int Int)`.  `sq` is the two-wire case that used
# to need a packing and a strength, and now needs neither.

def sq with Frame = dup ; *
def poly with Frame = dup ; _ dup ; _ _ (_ 1 ; +) ; _ * ; +
def wiring with Frame = dup ; _ 1 ... ; _ + ; swap ; _ drop
#   each of these is `Int =Frame Recursive> Int`

def sqOut in Frame = sq ; observe
def polyOut in Frame = poly ; observe
def wiringOut in Frame = wiring ; observe

"-- transport, at the audit's own three-row frame [1, 2, 3] --" ; print
sqOut     ; print       # 14 — [1, 4, 9]      summed
polyOut   ; print       # 26 — [3, 8, 15]     summed: x + x·(x+1)
wiringOut ; print       # 9  — [2, 3, 4]      summed: dup, +1, swap, drop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Frame-level words — everything a functor may NOT do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# These take columns and give columns.  They are written once, here,
# because each of them moves, drops or reorders rows, and the index
# reading says a transported word cannot.

## keep the rows a Bool column marks.  A MASK, not a predicate: the
## filter a row program would want is `row ⇒ (row | •)`, and a sum
## under transport needs a choice slot the Doctrine does not have
## (design-macros.md, 2026-09-14).  The mask sidesteps it — the
## deciding is a column, and the dropping is frame-level.
def keep with Recursive =
    (mask rows -> mask ; unList ; (nil \
      | (b bs -> rows ; unList ; (nil \
          | (x xs -> (bs xs ; keep) ; (r -> b (x r ; cons) r ; select))) ; merge)) ; merge)

## does a column contain this value?
def elem = (x col -> col ; [(seen y -> seen (x y ; equals) ; or)] false ... ; fold)

## the distinct values of a column, in first-appearance order
def distinct =
    col ->
    col ; [(seen x -> (x seen ; elem) seen (x seen ; cons) ; select)] nil ... ; fold
        ; reverse

## the rows whose key equals k
def rowsAt = (k keys rows -> (keys ; [(y -> k y ; equals)] ... ; map) rows ; keep)

## a group is a key and the frame of rows under it — a COLUMN OF
## FRAMES, which is what `groupBy` hands back
data Group(k, a) = (key: k, part: List(a))

def groupBy =
    keys rows ->
    (keys ; distinct) ; [(k -> k (k keys rows ; rowsAt) ; Group)] ... ; map

## insertion sort over `Box(key row)` elements, by a quoted router on
## keys — `[flt?]` for ascending Floats, `[lt?]` for Ints
def keyOf = (e -> e ; unBox ; (k r -> k))
def rowOf = (e -> e ; unBox ; (k r -> r))

def insertPair with Recursive =
    (p e l -> l ; unList ; (e ; single \
      | (y r -> ((e ; keyOf) (y ; keyOf) ; p ... ; ev ; verdict) \
                  (e l ; cons) (y (p e r ; insertPair) ; cons) ; select)) ; merge)

def sortPairs with Recursive =
    (p l -> l ; unList ; (nil | (x r -> p x (p r ; sortPairs) ; insertPair)) ; merge)

## reorder a frame by a key column
def sortBy = (p keys rows -> ((keys rows ; zip) ; p ... ; sortPairs) ; [rowOf] ... ; map)

## inner join on a key: every left row beside every right row that
## shares its key.  Rows with no partner are dropped, on both sides.
def join =
    keys rows others otherRows ->
    ((keys rows ; zip)
       ; [(e -> e ; unBox
                  ; (k a -> (k others otherRows ; rowsAt)
                              ; [(b -> a b ; Box)] ... ; map))] ... ; map)
    ; concat

## the aggregations — ordinary folds over a column.  `count` is the
## prelude's `len`: a frame IS its rows, so the list word already is
## the aggregation, and a second spelling would be a second name for
## one thing.
def sumF  = [fadd] 0.0 ... ; fold
def meanF = (col -> (col ; sumF) (col ; len ; toFloat) ; fdiv)

## ONE-OFFS, outside a scope: `[f] rows ; column` embeds a row word and
## runs it.  It is written in the model's words on purpose — `column`
## denotes the same function `map` does, and that it does is `embed`'s
## defining equation rather than a coincidence.
def column in Frame = (f rows -> (f ; embed) rows ; runCol)

## join a column of strings with a separator, and print a frame under a
## header the CALLER supplies.  The header is runtime data — a
## `List(Str)` — because column names are words, not types.
def joinWith =
    sep col ->
    col ; unList
        ; ("" | (x r -> x (r ; [(acc y -> (acc sep ; cat) y ; cat)] "" ... ; fold) ; cat))
        ; merge

def printFrame =
    header rows ->
    (" | " header ; joinWith) ; print
    ; rows ; [(r -> " | " r ; joinWith)] ... ; map ; printAll

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. The loader — one DECLARATION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# A CSV's header IS a presentation: it declares a data type.  So this
# line is the whole loader (2026-09-15, MANUAL §8):

table Trades = "examples/data/trades.csv"

# The LOADER — Haskell, at the same IO boundary `import` already uses —
# resolves the path by the import rule, reads the WHOLE file at check
# time, and writes Braid text that is included exactly as an imported
# file's declarations are.  What it wrote here is what stood in this
# section by hand until 2026-09-15:
#
#   data Trades = (sym: Str, px: Float, qty: Int)
#     sym : Trades ⇒ Str   px : Trades ⇒ Float   qty : Trades ⇒ Int
#     — the column types READ OFF THE DATA: `Int` if every cell is an
#       Int literal, else `Float` if every cell is a Float or an Int
#       literal, else `Str`.  The whole file is read, so nothing is
#       guessed about a row that was not looked at.
#   headerTrades : • ⇒ List(Str)
#     — the header line verbatim, runtime data, for `printFrame`
#   loadTrades : Str =IO Recursive> (List(Trades) | Str)
#     — a bad row goes to the miss track carrying its LINE NUMBER,
#       counted in the file (the header is line 1), so the shape is the
#       ordinary railway and the whole load is `sequence` over the rows.
#       Its helpers are named `Trades@…`, the compiler's spelling, which
#       source may not write: a table puts exactly two words in scope.
#
# Nothing about `table` runs.  The generated text is the only thing that
# does, and it re-reads the file — so a CSV that changed since the check
# puts its first bad row on the miss track, by line number.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. The row programs, and the words that present them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

## THE ROW PROGRAM.  `px qty` is a two-wire stage — two column names
## side by side — and it embeds as itself, at two wires.  No `List`, no
## index, no loop, and nothing here knows there is more than one row.
def notional with Frame = dup ; px qty ; _ toFloat ; fmul
#   notional : Trades =Frame Recursive> Float

## running one on a frame, outside the scope
def notionalOf = (rows -> notional rows ; runCol)

## the mask, as a row program run by `column`: a FILTER would want
## `Trades ⇒ (Trades | •)`, and a sum has no slot in the Doctrine
def big?     = (t -> (t ; qty) 100 ; gte? ; verdict)
def bigOnes  = (rows -> ([big?] rows ; column) rows ; keep)

## group by symbol, total the notional in each group
data Total = (tsym: Str, tval: Float)
def totalOf  = (g -> (g ; key) (g ; part ; sumF) ; Total)
def totalsOf =
    rows ->
    (([sym] rows ; column) (rows ; notionalOf) ; groupBy) ; [totalOf] ... ; map

## reorder by a key column — ascending, since `flt?` hits on less-than
def sortedTotals = (ts -> [flt?] ([tval] ts ; column) ts ; sortBy)

## a second, tiny frame to join against.  NFLX has no trade and TSLA no
## sector, so an inner join drops one row from each side.
def sectorSyms  = "AAPL" "MSFT" "GOOG" "NFLX" ; pack
def sectorNames = "Hardware" "Software" "Ads" "Media" ; pack
def withSector  = (ts -> ([tsym] ts ; column) ts sectorSyms sectorNames ; join)

## cells — a row becomes the `List(Str)` the printer takes.
##
## `tradeCells` is DERIVED (2026-09-16), and this is the whole of it: a
## row printer is a function of the DECLARATION and of nothing else, so
## writing one by hand is writing out what `table Trades` already said.
## `declOf` hands back that declaration as data, `cellsFor` (the
## prelude) turns it into the printer's `Code` — `(r -> (r ; sym)
## (r ; px ; toStr) (r ; qty ; toStr) ; pack)`, read off the field names
## and their types — and `evalAs` runs that Code against a WITNESS whose
## arrow it must meet.  The output below is byte for byte what the
## hand-written line printed.
##
## The witness is one column wide: it says `Trades ⇒ List(Str)` and
## nothing more, which is exactly the expectation the derived printer
## has to satisfy.  What is missing is a way to DECLARE a def from Code:
## a `functor` would splice this at ELABORATION and cost nothing at run
## time, but every `with` mints a receipt, and `Trades =Cells> List(Str)`
## no longer fits `embed`'s `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩`.  That gap is stage 8's
## (MANUAL §12, design-macros.md 2026-09-16).
def cellsWitness = (r -> (r ; sym) ; single)
def tradesCode   = "Trades" ; declOf ; ((d -> d ; cellsFor) | drop ; nil) ; merge
def tradeCells   =
    t -> [cellsWitness] (tradesCode) (t) ; evalAs ; ((c -> c) | (e x -> e ; single)) ; merge
def totalCells  = (t -> (t ; tsym) (t ; tval ; toStr) ; pack)
def joinCells   = (e -> e ; unBox ; (t sec -> (t ; tsym) (t ; tval ; toStr) sec ; pack))
def symNotional =
    rows ->
    (([sym] rows ; column) (rows ; notionalOf) ; zip)
      ; [(e -> e ; unBox ; (s v -> s (v ; toStr) ; pack))] ... ; map

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. A FRAME-LEVEL LAW — one that is not a functor law
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The doctrine audits the model.  `keep` is not part of the model, so
# nothing audits it; here is the law it owes, stated as a program.
#
#     keep with an all-true mask is the identity
#
# That is a real theorem about `keep` and it is decidable at any frame
# by `eq?`, since a `Trades` row is three scalars.  The other one worth
# wanting — `groupBy` then concatenating the groups is a PERMUTATION of
# the frame — is NOT statable this way: "is a permutation" needs a
# canonical order on rows, which needs an ordering on rows, which this
# language does not have (`eq?` is structural, `lt?` is Int).  What IS
# statable is its shadow: grouping preserves the ROW COUNT, no row
# lost, none duplicated.  Stating the shadow and saying what the full
# claim would cost is the honest position.

def allTrue     = (rows -> [(x -> true)] rows ; column)
def keepIdentity = (rows -> ((rows ; allTrue) rows ; keep) rows ; eq? ; verdict)
def groupSizes  = (gs -> [(g -> g ; part ; len)] gs ; column)
def groupsCount =
    rows -> ((([sym] rows ; column) rows ; groupBy) ; groupSizes ; sum) (rows ; len)
              ; equals
def says = (b -> b "true" "false" ; select)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. The demo
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

"examples/data/trades.csv" ; loadTrades
; (pass | (e -> e ; print ; nil)) ; merge
; (trades ->

    "" ; print
    "-- the frame, as loaded --" ; print
    headerTrades ([tradeCells] trades ; column) ; printFrame
    ("rows: " (trades ; len ; toStr) ; cat) ; print
    #   12 rows, four symbols, three of them repeated

    "" ; print
    "-- keep qty >= 100, and the notional of what is left --" ; print
    (trades ; bigOnes)
    ; (big ->
        ("sym" "notional" ; pack) (big ; symNotional) ; printFrame
        #   AAPL | 19050.0     = 190.5 * 100
        #   AAPL | 38400.0     = 192.0 * 200
        #   MSFT | 61800.0     = 412.0 * 150
        #   MSFT | 40850.0     = 408.5 * 100
        ("rows: " (big ; len ; toStr) ; cat) ; print          # 4
        ("mean: " (big ; notionalOf ; meanF ; toStr) ; cat) ; print)
        #   160100 / 4 = 40025.0, and every term is a dyadic rational,
        #   so the sum and the quotient are both exact

    "" ; print
    "-- group by sym, sum the notional, then sort ascending --" ; print
    (trades ; totalsOf)
    ; (totals ->
        ("sym" "notional" ; pack) ([totalCells] totals ; column) ; printFrame
        #   AAPL | 82347.5     = 19050 + 38400 + 9437.5 + 15460
        #   MSFT | 123150.0    = 20500 + 61800 + 40850
        #   GOOG | 22466.25    = 10518.75 + 3487.5 + 8460
        #   TSLA | 12450.0     = 2500 + 9950
        "" ; print
        ("sym" "notional" ; pack) ([totalCells] (totals ; sortedTotals) ; column)
          ; printFrame
        #   TSLA | 12450.0 , GOOG | 22466.25 , AAPL | 82347.5 , MSFT | 123150.0

        "" ; print
        "-- inner join with a sector frame --" ; print
        ("sym" "notional" "sector" ; pack) \
          ([joinCells] (totals ; withSector) ; column) ; printFrame)
        #   AAPL | 82347.5 | Hardware
        #   MSFT | 123150.0 | Software
        #   GOOG | 22466.25 | Ads
        #   — NFLX has no trade and TSLA no sector, so both are dropped

    "" ; print
    "-- the frame-level laws, run --" ; print
    ("keep with an all-true mask is the identity: " \
       (trades ; keepIdentity ; says) ; cat) ; print          # true
    ("groupBy preserves the row count: " \
       (trades ; groupsCount ; says) ; cat) ; print)          # true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. A second table, and the SCHEMA form
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `examples/data/sectors.csv` has a header cell that is not a word —
# `Ticker Name` — and a weight column whose cells all look like Ints.
# Left to itself the declaration would read
#
#     data Sector = (ticker_Name: Str, sector: Str, weight: Int)
#
# by the sanitizing rule: each space, tab and `-` in a header cell
# becomes `_`, and the first letter is lowercased.  Neither the name nor
# the type is wanted, so the SCHEMA form writes both.  There is ONE such
# form, not one for renaming and one for ascribing, because renaming a
# column and giving it a type are the same act: naming it.

table Sector(ticker: Str, sector: Str, weight: Float) = "examples/data/sectors.csv"

def sectorCells = (r -> (r ; ticker) (r ; sector) (r ; weight ; toStr) ; pack)

"" ; print
"-- a second table, declared with a written schema --" ; print
"examples/data/sectors.csv" ; loadSector
; (pass | (e -> e ; print ; nil)) ; merge
; (rows ->
    headerSector ([sectorCells] rows ; column) ; printFrame
    #   the header printed is the FILE's line, verbatim — `Ticker Name`
    #   included.  The WORDS are `ticker`, `sector` and `weight`: the
    #   header is a presentation, and the schema is what the program
    #   calls it.
    ("weights: " (([weight] rows ; column) ; sumF ; toStr) ; cat) ; print)
    #   100.0, a Float, because the schema said Float.  The cells are
    #   `40`, `35` and `25`, which sniff as Int; `fadd` would not take
    #   them, and `sumF` is a Float fold.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. What is NOT built, plainly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INDEX IS IMPLICIT.  A frame here is `List(row)` and its keys are
# `0 … n−1`.  The `Fn⟨I ⇒ row⟩` reading with an explicit key set would
# make `join` a map union rather than a nested scan, and would let two
# frames share an index without being the same length.  It needs a key
# type in the carrier — `Col(i, a, b)` — and that is a three-parameter
# hom-object, which the Doctrine's `k(_, _)` does not admit.
#
# NO COLUMN STORE.  `Col(a, b)` is a function between columns and the
# frame is stored as rows.  The column-store representation — the
# frame as a STACK of columns rather than a list of rows — would be a
# second model of the same theory, and then `transformation Columnar :
# RowStore ⇒ ColStore` is an ordinary declaration the checker decides.
# It waits on a stack-shaped carrier (design-macros.md, "not admitted:
# a stack-shaped embedding").
#
# NO FILTER INSIDE A SCOPE.  A row program that decides whether its row
# survives is `row ⇒ (row | •)`, and transporting a SUM needs a choice
# slot in the Doctrine — ArrowChoice' `left`.  `keep` sidesteps it: the
# deciding is a column (a functor may compute a Bool), the dropping is
# frame-level.
#
# THE HEADER IS RUNTIME DATA.  `printFrame` takes the names as a
# `List(Str)` from the caller, and `headerTrades` is where they now come
# from — the CSV's own header line, verbatim, read at check time and
# emitted as a definition.  Nothing above the loader changed when the
# hand-written one became a `table` line.
